#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172946} :activity/id #uuid "811b6c3b-bd9c-5c41-8fe9-0970ca3fedb9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433 :topic/id #uuid "f4f8286a-494e-5683-85be-d6899a52bb51" :topic/math-academy-id 3289} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173179 :tutorial/content "The **gamma function** $\\Gamma(z)$ extends the factorial function to positive real numbers and parts of the complex plane.\n\nIn particular, for any natural number $n \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n) = (n-1)!\n$$\n\nIf the complex number $z$ satisfies $\\textrm{Re}(z) > 0,$ then the gamma function can be defined as the improper integral\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will only consider cases where $z$ is a positive real number." :tutorial/id #uuid "9fea375f-e55b-5c47-a7ae-c2ef9f860a2c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5337 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4bee0881-4e23-5850-9acc-e043caf9668c" :step/math-academy-id 33407 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172952}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172064 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171870 :question/id #uuid "62da71f1-5a3a-509a-a68e-0596f88c4ae4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8951"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59f9abf1-17db-5717-8660-a531ef00b15c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487302}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function for a Positive Integer"} :step/id #uuid "96bce02d-680a-5dd7-a5f2-34c1e5553c65" :step/math-academy-id 33409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173180 :tutorial/content "To evaluate the gamma function at positive integer multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z), \\qquad \\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "a2e308ea-2921-5cda-bb43-b4914e8efaf1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5338 :tutorial/title "The Recursive Formula and the Value of the Gamma Function at One-Half"} :step/id #uuid "5e5c30dd-3fe8-5761-b1e6-76ab3ced125d" :step/math-academy-id 33410 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172065 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171871 :question/id #uuid "5d084f86-9874-5bdd-a416-dba742d7e4e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8952"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8de43f-dc61-5bbe-8b84-b8aa0be19146" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050380}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487391}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function at a Multiple of One-Half"} :step/id #uuid "bf112a76-b1a6-5da1-b844-9eb0f13eb3f1" :step/math-academy-id 33411 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173181 :tutorial/content "Previously, we used the following relationship between the gamma function and the factorial:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n) = (n-1)!\n$$\n\nThis relationship holds for any natural number $n \\geq 1.$ Now, let's prove it.\n\nFirst, we start with the following identity, which we proved earlier:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z)\n$$\n\nFor any natural number $n \\geq 1,$ the above identity states the following:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n+1) = n \\Gamma(n)\n$$\n\nNow, let's repeatedly expand out the right-hand side, noticing that most of the terms can be grouped into a factorial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(n+1) & =nΓ(n) \\\\  & =n\\cdot \\overset{Γ(n)}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-1)\\cdot Γ(n-1)}} \\\\  & =n\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot \\overset{Γ(n-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-2)\\cdot Γ(n-2)}} \\\\  & =… \\\\  & ={{n\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot (n-2)\\cdot …\\cdot 1}_{⏟}}_{n!}\\cdot Γ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n+1) = n! \\cdot \\Gamma(1).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $\\Gamma(1),$ we can use the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(1) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n+1) = n!\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "66ce2df9-01d6-5873-b9e6-bb9e2a7d35af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5339 :tutorial/title "Proving the Relationship Between the Gamma Function and the Factorial"} :step/id #uuid "272794b0-bed3-5ee3-a248-3dc313b1fd31" :step/math-academy-id 33412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172951}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173182 :tutorial/content "Previously, we used the fact that $\\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\pi}.$ To prove this, we can directly compute the value of $\\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)$ using the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{-1/2} e^{-t} \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe simplify this integral using the substitution $t = u^2,$ which implies $\\text{d}t = 2u \\, \\text{d}u.$ Also, note that $u \\to 0^+$ as $t \\to 0^+$ and $u \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nt^{-1/2} e^{-t} \\, \\text{d}t = (u^2)^{-1/2} e^{-u^2} \\cdot 2u \\, \\text{d}u = e^{-u^2} \\cdot 2 \\, \\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\Gamma\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{-1/2} e^{-t} \\, \\text{d}t = 2 \\int_0^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nThis is directly related to the well-known **Gaussian integral**:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nSince the integrand $e^{-u^2}$ is even (i.e., symmetric about the $y$-axis), we have\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-u^2} \\, \\text{d}u = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sqrt{\\pi}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into our expression for the Gamma function, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\Gamma\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = 2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sqrt{\\pi} = \\sqrt{\\pi}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79026717-bbea-524a-a1e0-01ef7570bfac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5340 :tutorial/title "Evaluating the Gamma Function at One-Half"} :step/id #uuid "7a3ab1f8-c83c-5b32-87fd-ef21fb859957" :step/math-academy-id 33413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171872 :question/id #uuid "aab1e422-0ac4-59b7-bed9-5ce75b4fe22d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9033"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432698}] :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function"} :step/id #uuid "09c6442a-deed-570b-8939-d804e74acc5c" :step/math-academy-id 33743 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173224 :tutorial/content "Here, we'll prove the recursive formula for the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z)\n$$\n\nThe formula comes from applying integration by parts to the integral in the definition of the gamma function. By definition,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt, \\qquad\\textrm{Re}(z) > 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(z+1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{z}{e}^{-t}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-z{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{{t}^{z}}{{e}^{t}})-{(-\\frac{{t}^{z}}{{e}^{t}})|}_{t=0}+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }z{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0-0+z{{{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t}_{⏟}}_{Γ(z)} \\\\  & =zΓ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68b4463b-d790-5bf1-9972-da35d3818a8b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6699 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Recursive Formula"} :step/id #uuid "b3e9fad8-09f6-5769-9d53-ab348d94359e" :step/math-academy-id 40566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172950}}] :activity/title "The Gamma Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172142}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487412} :activity/id #uuid "364c21bd-4c9d-517c-904f-c5dd46711403" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433 :topic/id #uuid "f4f8286a-494e-5683-85be-d6899a52bb51" :topic/math-academy-id 3289} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487412 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a77bfb25-095b-5057-aba8-6f268f5f742f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168244} {:answer/id #uuid "ae77212e-2a0c-5870-a919-ed167b91f235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168245} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6195c6-eda7-592f-badd-965c6e91f474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168246} {:answer/id #uuid "4275d640-6db8-51ce-bc7f-8b264307f6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168247} {:answer/id #uuid "65656ef1-3e07-56e8-8556-fed7a19dd1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168248} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5b5e92c-1cc2-5285-b792-14856967269c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f5a44a3-4ea9-54a0-a167-f690d2f22615" :question/math-academy-id "q-105736" :question/problem "Evaluate $Γ(5).$" :question/worked-solution "For any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(n)=(n-1)!.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nΓ(5)=4!=24.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2329b882-b9d8-579a-ac94-6f8fdcc055e1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487440}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3372a2b1-d89c-5ea5-ae2d-dc597ba5d5b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\sqrt{\\pi }}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486704} {:answer/id #uuid "580968ec-8d67-5eee-8ed5-dc153c668ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486787} {:answer/id #uuid "636f9395-da30-5934-b0e7-87be6c2499c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486812} {:answer/id #uuid "93cccf53-80db-5897-ab51-a8e524781991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486919} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8079d4-cfe9-5db0-8ed9-f1196344fbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486919} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa87497-13d4-5b82-adae-cac5f16dbe31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dcd3058f-0b72-5c3c-b3c8-705d9f7da27d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107857" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,Γ(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "32dda758-1c10-5e03-9107-ca2708d04732" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487456}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487438 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00754836-f0cd-50e5-aa02-7d189b80edae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486596} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc959a4-5056-5435-b552-7ae39524df65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486827} {:answer/id #uuid "731dc778-4a1e-5474-afe6-d3d089ddf682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486843} {:answer/id #uuid "7d43dd58-4629-52b1-8528-64bea432b08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486866} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ee9600-62e5-57c5-a87c-047a37f4904a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486596} :answer-field/id #uuid "68ee2d15-0779-5b6d-a0a3-58d2b6c1ee1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1397b57e-0338-5e52-920b-2086a4adac56" :question/math-academy-id "q-107861" :question/problem "$2\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2})+Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\nΓ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi },\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{5}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,Γ(\\frac{5}{2})+Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=2\\sqrt{\\pi }.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c203f272-1a4b-54ae-b93f-7712948cad73"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487440 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "206fa060-e5b7-5b92-9899-8411d25fd4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486659} {:answer/id #uuid "98d40466-0fee-5000-9a4e-74165fc45aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486940} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff974d8-7678-510e-a50c-d042422993ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40\\,320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486956} {:answer/id #uuid "c305fd9c-6218-528d-9a5f-49bc8d25aa53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,080" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487025} {:answer/id #uuid "e98f9aaf-ae44-57d2-b3aa-a7763efa9ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486659} :answer-field/id #uuid "22c5e926-ac1f-5f05-944d-e1b4e4b09520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1f98d6c6-9c7c-5432-abf6-c7066e09f238" :question/math-academy-id "q-107851" :question/problem "Evaluate $Γ(8).$" :question/worked-solution "For any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(n)=(n-1)!.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nΓ(8)=7!=5\\,040.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cb0e3217-8975-5e32-8519-6af77622bccf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487416}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487456 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0998b024-6768-582f-945a-d5609ee9f1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486614} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb28415-d03e-5dc3-8cb7-b89810a39b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486680} {:answer/id #uuid "8908fc27-a7eb-5475-8abd-39bad6c188d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486891} {:answer/id #uuid "961cc474-dd53-5341-baee-0b7c843c8e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486927} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a2c475-4407-520d-aa2c-30700dd13b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486680} :answer-field/id #uuid "58b55a76-73d7-558c-b98f-2fa3e367ce2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df3869ef-efb9-5577-906d-3eff34aa9ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-107859" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fd8c07fe-0842-5d46-b2b5-014a37a09eba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487438}}] :activity/title "The Gamma Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486586}]}
